ite16†Frontier safety L72: \nL73: Science\nL74: ## Unlocking a new era of discovery with AI\nL75: \nL76: cite17†Explore science L77: \nL78: Breakthroughs\nL79: \nL80: cite18†AlphaFold Predict protein structures with high accuracy cite19†AlphaGenome Using AI to understand the human genome cite20†WeatherNext Fast and accurate AI weather forecasting cite21†AlphaEarth Map our planet in unprecedented detail cite22†AlphaEvolve Design advanced algorithms for math and applications in computing L81: \nL82: Learn more\nL83: cite23†Gemini for Science†ai.google cite24†Experimental Tools†labs.google cite25†Science Skills†antigravity.google L84: \nL85: About\nL86: ## Our mission is to build AI responsibly to benefit humanity\nL87: \nL88: cite26†About Google DeepMind L89: \nL90: Learn more\nL91: \nL92: cite29†Education cite30†Our National Partnerships for AI cite31†Accelerator programs cite32†The Podcast cite33†DeepMind Institute†institute.deepmind.com L93: \nL94: cite15†Responsibility Ensuring AI safety through proactive security, even against evolving threats cite27†News Discover our latest AI breakthroughs, projects, and updates cite28†Careers We’re looking for people who want to make a real, positive impact on the world L95: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L96: \nL97: cite36†Google DeepMind L98: \nL99: cite37†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite38†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI cite39†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google cite40†Google Research Explore our research†research.google L100: \nL101: Products and apps\nL102: cite35†Gemini app Chat with Gemini†gemini.google.com cite34†Google AI Studio Build with our next-gen AI models†aistudio.google.com cite41†Google Antigravity Our agentic development platform†antigravity.google L103: \nL104: cite0†Models L105: \nL106: cite10†Research L107: \nL108: cite17†Science L109: \nL110: cite26†About L111: \nL112: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L113: \nL114: February 11, 2026 Research\nL115: # Accelerating Mathematical and Scientific Discovery with Gemini Deep Think\nL116: \nL117: Thang Luong and Vahab Mirrokni\nL118: \nL119: cite42†Image: An abstract digital illustration representing Gemini Deep Think, featuring a glowing blue wireframe sphere intersected by an axis, surrounded by mathematical notation, code snippets, and streams of colored data particles against a dark background.†lh3.googleusercontent.com L120: \nL121: Your browser does not support the audio element.\nL122: \nL123: Listen to article 10 minutes\nL124: Under direction from expert mathematicians and scientists, Gemini Deep Think is solving professional research problems across mathematics, physics, and computer science\nL125: In the summer of 2025, an advanced version of Gemini Deep Think cite43†achieved†goo.gle Gold-medal standard at the International Mathematics Olympiad (IMO) and later, an updated version, cite44†obtained similar results at the International Collegiate Programming Contest. These results demonstrated the model could reason through some of the most challenging math and programming problems designed for students.\nL126: Since then, Gemini Deep Think mode has moved into science, engineering and enterprise workflows to tackle more complex, open-ended challenges.\nL127: In the last week, our teams published two papers (cite45†1†arxiv.org , cite46†2†arxiv.org ) detailing a cross-disciplinary effort to solve professional research problems using Gemini Deep Think mode. These results stem from deep collaboration between mathematicians, physicists, and computer scientists.\nL128: ## The Frontier of Pure Mathematics\nL129: \nL130: Unlike IMO problems, research-level mathematics requires advanced techniques from vast literature. While foundation models have large knowledge bases, data scarcity often leads to superficial understanding and hallucinations in advanced subjects.\nL131: To solve this, we built a math research agent (internally codenamed Aletheia), powered by Gemini Deep Think mode. It features a natural language verifier to identify flaws in candidate solutions and enable an iterative process of generating and revising solutions. Crucially, this agent can admit failure to solve a problem, a key feature that improved the efficiency for researchers.\nL132: Additionally, the research agent uses Google Search and web browsing to navigate complex research, preventing spurious citations and computational inaccuracies when synthesizing published literature.\nL133: cite47†Image: Flowchart titled \"Aletheia: Powered by Gemini Deep Think\" illustrating a multi-step solution verification process.\nL134: A central linear path moves from \"Problem\" to \"Generator,\" then \"Candidate solution,\" then \"Verifier,\" and finally \"Final output.\" The Verifier acts as a decision point with three feedback loops: Correct: Moves directly to \"Final output.\" Minor fixes needed: Routes back through a \"Reviser\" to update the \"Candidate solution.\" Critically flawed: Triggers a red dashed line back to the \"Generator\" to restart the process.†storage.googleapis.com cite48†Image: Flowchart titled \"Aletheia: Powered by Gemini Deep Think\" illustrating a multi-step solution verification process.\nL135: A central linear path moves from \"Problem\" to \"Generator,\" then \"Candidate solution,\" then \"Verifier,\" and finally \"Final output.\" The Verifier acts as a decision point with three feedback loops: Correct: Moves directly to \"Final output.\" Minor fixes needed: Routes back through a \"Reviser\" to update the \"Candidate solution.\" Critically flawed: Triggers a red dashed line back to the \"Generator\" to restart the process.†storage.googleapis.com L136: Overview of Aletheia, a math research agent powered by Deep Think that can iteratively generate, verify, and revise for research-level math problems.\nL137: Since achieving IMO Gold-medal standard in July 2025, Gemini Deep Think has progressed rapidly, scoring up to 90% on the IMO-ProofBench Advanced cite49†test†imobench.github.io as inference-time compute scales. We demonstrated that the scaling law continues to hold as we progress beyond Olympiad level into PhD-level exercises (per our internal FutureMath Basic benchmark). Notably, Aletheia demonstrated that higher reasoning quality can be achieved at a lower inference-time compute.\nL138: cite50†Image: Line graph titled \"IMO-ProofBench Advanced (Olympiad level)\" comparing the performance of two versions of Gemini Deep Think against a benchmark named Aletheia. The y-axis shows Score percentage (30% to 90%), and the x-axis shows Inference-Time Compute on a logarithmic scale .The January 2026 version (light blue) consistently outperforms the July 2025 version (dark blue), with both showing a steady upward trend as compute increases.\nL139: The January 2026 version peaks at approximately 90% nearly reaching the Aletheia benchmark (green star) positioned just above 90%.†storage.googleapis.com cite51†Image: Line graph titled \"IMO-ProofBench Advanced (Olympiad level)\" comparing the performance of two versions of Gemini Deep Think against a benchmark named Aletheia.\nL140: The y-axis shows Score percentage (30% to 90%), and the x-axis shows Inference-Time Compute on a logarithmic scale .The January 2026 version (light blue) consistently outperforms the July 2025 version (dark blue), with both showing a steady upward trend as compute increases. The January 2026 version peaks at approximately 90% nearly reaching the Aletheia benchmark (green star) positioned just above 90%.†storage.googleapis.com L141: The latest advanced version of Deep Think, as of Jan 2026, has significantly outperformed the IMO-Gold version (Jul 2025) on Olympiad-level problems. Aletheia makes further leaps in terms of reasoning quality with lower inference-time compute. All results were graded by human experts.\nL142: cite52†Image: Line graph titled \"(b) FutureMath Basic (Ph.D level exercises)\" plotting the score of Gemini Deep Think (Jan 2026) against increasing Inference-Time Compute on a logarithmic scale. The y-axis represents Score percentage (0% to 45%). The performance starts at 0% and shows a volatile upward trend, hitting an early peak of 30% before fluctuating and eventually climbing to a final high of approximately 38%.\nL143: A green star representing \"Aletheia\" is marked as a benchmark at roughly 46%, remaining significantly above the model's highest performance reached.†storage.googleapis.com cite53†Image: Line graph titled \"(b) FutureMath Basic (Ph.D level exercises)\" plotting the score of Gemini Deep Think (Jan 2026) against increasing Inference-Time Compute on a logarithmic scale. The y-axis represents Score percentage (0% to 45%).\nL144: The performance starts at 0% and shows a volatile upward trend, hitting an early peak of 30% before fluctuating and eventually climbing to a final high of approximately 38%. A green star representing \"Aletheia\" is marked as a benchmark at roughly 46%, remaining significantly above the model's highest performance reached.†storage.googleapis.com L145: The inference-time scaling law also transfers to PhD-level exercises.\nL146: \nL147: For research-level math, Aletheia has already enabled several advancements, produced via varying levels of autonomous research:\nL148:   * Reliable autonomous research. A research paper (cite54†Feng26†arxiv.org ) generated by AI without any human intervention, which calculates certain structure constants in arithmetic geometry called eigenweights.\nL149:   * AI-guided collaboration. A research paper (cite55†LeeSeo26†arxiv.org ) demonstrating human-AI collaboration in proving bounds on systems of interacting particles called independent sets.\nL150:   * An extensive semi-autonomous evaluation (cite56†Feng et al., 2026b†arxiv.org ) of 700 open problems on Bloom’s Erdős Conjectures cite57†database†www.erdosproblems.com , including autonomous solutions to four open questions listed there. On Erdős-1051, our model autonomously solved and helped lead to a generalization reported in a research paper (cite58†BKKKZ26†arxiv.org ).\nL151: The agent also contributed intermediate propositions on two further papers, (cite59†FYZ26†arxiv.org ) and (cite60†ACGKMP26†arxiv.org ). It is also of note that there has been prior cite61†work†arxiv.org using Gemini for research-level math at a smaller scale in terms of collaborations and the number of problems tackled.\nL152: Following extensive discussions with the mathematical community, we suggest a taxonomy to classify AI-assisted mathematics research by significance and degree of AI contribution - contributing to the wider discussion on responsible documentation, evaluation and communication of AI-generated results. Level 2 (“publishable quality”) works have been submitted to reputable journals. Currently, we do not claim any Level 3 (“Major Advance”) and Level 4 (“Landmark Breakthrough”) results.\nL153: cite62†Image: A table titled \"Classification of all AI-assisted mathematics results encompassed in this work\" categorizes research by novelty and collaboration type.Level 0 (Autonomous): Erdős-652, 654, 1040 (Feng et al., 2026b).Level 1 (Autonomous): Erdős-1051 (Feng et al., 2026b).Level 2 (Human + AI): Complexity Bounds (ACGKMP26) and Arithmetic Volumes (FYZ26).Level 2 (Collaboration): Independence Polynomials (LeeSeo26) and Generalized Erdős-1051 (BKKKZ26).Level 2 (Autonomous): Eigenweights (Feng26).Levels 3 and 4 are currently empty.†storage.googleapis.com cite63†Image: A table titled \"Classification of all AI-assisted mathematics results encompassed in this work\" categorizes research by novelty and collaboration type.Level 0 (Autonomous): Erdős-652, 654, 1040 (Feng et al., 2026b).Level 1 (Autonomous): Erdős-1051 (Feng et al., 2026b).Level 2 (Human + AI): Complexity Bounds (ACGKMP26) and Arithmetic Volumes (FYZ26).Level 2 (Collaboration): Independence Polynomials (LeeSeo26) and Generalized Erdős-1051 (BKKKZ26).Level 2 (Autonomous): Eigenweights (Feng26).Levels 3 and 4 are currently empty.†storage.googleapis.com L154: Classification of all AI-assisted mathematics results encompassed in this work. *Works listed as Level 2 in this table have been submitted for publications.\nL155: \nL156: Prompts and model outputs are available cite64†here†github.com . For discussions on AI contributions, our “Human-AI Interaction card”, and community impact, see our cite45†paper†arxiv.org .\nL157: ## Expanding to Physics and Computer Science\n"
